+# Verified retained subscription loan ownership
+
+Executor-owned loans now use the existing owner-aware `handle_message` dispatch.
+The callback's shared pointers own the actual loan, with no message copy added.
+Its final strong owner returns the loan and releases its RCL subscription handle.
+The ownership chain is: message deleter -> RCL subscription -> RCL node -> Context.
+
+RCL node and subscription deleters explicitly release captured parents at strong
+owner exhaustion, so weak references cannot keep native entities alive. C++
+subscription destruction unregisters callbacks before destroying function storage.
+Native handles can remain alive while messages are retained, even after C++
+wrapper destruction; shutdown stops communication while retained message data
+remains valid. Last-owner release can happen on another thread.
+
+No vtable or object layout changed. Executor dispatch now uses `handle_message`
+for owned loans; direct raw `handle_loaned_message` calls remain caller-managed.
+This behavioral change and the new parent lifetime are pre-push review items.
+
+TDD and real RK3588A evidence:
+
+- `retained_loan_red_20260908` verified the forwarding RCL return interceptor,
+  then B recorded `CPP_RETAIN_HELD 1 1`: an early return. The test rejected it
+  before reading freed storage. RED report SHA256: `960dfa46f93279fb0fc502bdf1c3f64da04658e88ecedd882a28821bba49ae82`.
+- `retained_loan_green_20260908_02` verified ten held messages, subscription
+  callback removal, Context retention, cross-thread release and weak cleanup.
+- `retained_exc_reg_20260908` separately verified two non-retaining callback
+  exceptions and ten normal returns on the same RCLCPP library.
+- Final `retained_diag_20260908` also read all held payloads after subscription
+  destruction and Context shutdown, and the final payload after releasing the
+  other four. Both peers released all five loans exactly once, with no cleanup
+  errors; graph phases, CLI inventories and real process exits passed.
+- Seven host contracts, 16 retained-loan receipt adversaries, ten C++ loan
+  adversaries, 11 graph adversaries and 18 generic checks passed. The baseline
+  receipt suite passed 11 cases.
+
+RCLCPP DSO: `b08f86941986e2727665b9d9e1739e82a8c4395988a07dd4d1ec27b01314d82c`.
+Final fixture: `f5c813d5d8d5edd22722b3918117906e458760c6b92c4419360f669ab954ca12`.
+Final report: `b284a97b5dffdc1daa066c943f459b2c6832724c0cd863e5b7027c7b1dde54d6`.
+
+The first ownership run closed a custom Context before the peer ACK gate;
+a two-sided ACK-before-teardown barrier corrected that test ordering without
+relaxing assertions. A later strengthened run (`retained_payload_20260908`)
+failed during MDDS publication before reaching content checks. Its evidence is
+preserved. The final run observed real queue pressure with new bounded MDDS
+diagnostics; reliable publish admission under that pressure is still being
+investigated. Passing loan lifetime checks does not close that separate issue.
+
+Reproduce from the ROS meta workspace using a fresh `MDDS_RUN_ID`,
+`MDDS_ROS_CPP_LOANS=1`, `MDDS_ROS_RETAINED_LOANS=1`,
+`MDDS_ROS_CLI_BATCH=graph_waiters`, `MDDS_ROS_PROFILE_MODE=implicit`, and
+`MDDS_ROS_RCLCPP_LIBRARY=build_ohos/rclcpp/librclcpp.so`, then run
+`bash scripts/run_mdds_broker_ros.sh`. Use `check_retained_loans.py` for adversaries.
+
+Seven unsupported RMW APIs, reliable-admission investigation, unified release
+validation and pre-push agentic-review remain open. No full-release claim is made.
